extraction_notes: "Rifts World Book 18: Mystic Russia pp.126-127, read from the text layer. A DECLARED COPY. Printed 126 does not define a class: it says the Russian Line Walker ''is fundamentally the same as the North American version described in the Rifts RPG, page 83. The only substantive difference is the selection of spells'', and then replaces item 8, Initial Spell Knowledge. That is the shape BOOK-INGEST-AUDIT F25 exists for, and the precedent is on this very parent: ley-line-rifter carries copy_of against ley-line-walker for RUE printed 118''s ''Same as the Ley Line Walker''. Nothing in this app composes one class from another, so the copy is FULL and copy_of records the relationship; regression.mjs then holds the pair together on every key outside the except list, against a database rebuilt from the repo. THE EXCEPT LIST WAS DERIVED BY DIFFING THE TWO PARSED ROWS, never typed - a stale except is its own defect and the sweep refuses one. A VARIANT COULD NOT CARRY THIS: VARIANT_OVERRIDES in js/parser.js lists attribute dice, pools, bonuses, skill_overrides, skills_additional and related_skills_count, and NOT magic - which is the entire delta here. THE FOUR NATURE/BONE PICKS ARE A NAMED LIST, NOT A TRADITION GATE: spell_traditions_allowed is class-wide (js/leveling.js, ''class-wide, the same for every group''), so setting it would also let the four Wizard picks reach Nature and Bone rows, which the book does not do. The list is the 49 rows this catalog holds at tradition nature or bone and level 7 or below, read off production rather than off the book. The eight Wizard picks are TWO FROM EACH of levels 1-4 rather than eight from levels 1-4, which is why they are four groups. Per-level growth drops from the parent''s two spells to ONE, at the character''s own level. The totals match by coincidence worth noting: 8 + 4 is 12, the same starting count the standard Ley Line Walker has."
---

# Russian Ley Line Walker

## Lore

The Russians call him a sorcerer, though "sorcerer" covers every kind of
practitioner there, the way "wizard" does in the West. He is the traditional
wizard-type spell caster, and one of the most feared, because his spell casting
is very diverse and very powerful.

Mechanically he is the Ley Line Walker of the Rifts RPG. What Russia changes is
what he knows: eight common Wizard spells across the first four levels, and
four more drawn from the Nature Magic and Bone Magic of this book.

## GM Notes

**This is a declared copy, not a new class.** Printed 126 says so outright, and
replaces exactly one numbered item - Initial Spell Knowledge. Everything else,
from the ley line powers to the concealed body armour, is the North American
Ley Line Walker''s.

He tends to avoid ritual magic but can perform rituals when needed. Each new
level brings one more wizard spell, at his own level rather than two below it.
', 'published', 'data-script'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM imported_classes WHERE class_id = 'russian-ley-line-walker');


-- Read the result back rather than trusting the exit code. d1-apply prints
-- these, and a CR in the stored markdown means the checkout mangled the file.
SELECT class_id, name, status, length(markdown) AS bytes, instr(markdown, char(13)) > 0 AS has_cr
  FROM imported_classes WHERE class_id = 'russian-ley-line-walker';

-- Records this run. REQUIRED: the smoke test fails a data script that has no
-- footer, or whose footer names a different file.
INSERT INTO data_script_runs (filename) VALUES ('add-russian-ley-line-walker-class.sql');
